body:
Hello Carlos,

I was unit testing the function "get_date_from_commit_hash()" which looks like this:

```r
get_date_from_commit_hash <- function(git_log,p_commit_hash){
    return(git_log[commit_hash] == p_commit_hash]$author_datetimetz[1])
}
```

I'm testing to see that this function returns "NA" if I pass a table that doesn't have a "commit_hash" column. However, when I do, it still returns the correct date. I'm not sure whether this is expected behavior, and to be honest I'm not sure how it's doing that. I've found that if I change the name of the column to be inside quotation marks, then it acts as expected. For example, if the function was

```r
get_date_from_commit_hash <- function(git_log,p_commit_hash){
    return(git_log["commit_hash"] == p_commit_hash]$author_datetimetz[1])
}
```

then it returns "NA" since it cannot find the column "commit_hash". My test looks like this:

```r
test_that("NA returned when table doesn't have commit_hash column", {
    minimal_dt = data.table(author_datetimetz, "random_name" = commit_hash, file_pathname)
    output_datetime <- get_date_from_commit_hash(minimal_dt, commit_hash_to_find)
    expect_equal(is.na(output_datetime), TRUE)
})
```

I initialize some variables at the beginning of the test file such as "author_datetimetz", "commit_hash", and "file_pathname". Should I allow this test to fail? Or do I remake the test if this is expected behavior?
